1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,200
[Night. Market Street. A Moon almost twice its size hangs over the roofs, and the sea is in the street, lifting a parked car off its wheels.]

2
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,380
NARRATOR: By the hundred-and-twentieth night, the sea came up Market Street to meet it.

3
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:08,800
[Day 1, dusk. The whole of Quay Street is out on the cobbles, faces turned up to a Moon that is just a little too big.]

4
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:09,080
NARRATOR: The first night, we just thought it was beautiful.

5
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:12,500
[Title: the Moon swelling over the words.]

6
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:13,670
NARRATOR: I was ten. This is what the next two hundred days were like.

7
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:17,090
[Six in the morning. Nana Odile at the tide gauge on the quay wall with a worn navy ledger; Wren beside her in a yellow raincoat.]

8
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:17,255
NARRATOR: My Nana wrote down the high tide twice a day.

9
00:00:17,525 --> 00:00:20,290
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

10
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:20,170
NARRATOR: So had her mother. And hers.

11
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,590
[The open log: two neat rows that match the tide table, and today’s, written in as we watch: 1.7 metres.]

12
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:24,410
NARRATOR: Forty centimetres over the table. That's not right.

13
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:27,390
[The sea is slopping over the quay edge around the fish stall. The fishmonger, ankle-deep and cheerful.]

14
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:27,270
NARRATOR: Spring tide, Odile! Put your wellies on!

15
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,975
[Reverse: nana answers.]

16
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:29,855
NARRATOR: Not in my book it isn't.

17
00:00:30,125 --> 00:00:33,275
[Nana’s kitchen: the range, the dresser, the window on the harbour. Mum, in her orange sea jacket, hears her pager and is out of the door before the toast is.]

18
00:00:30,225 --> 00:00:33,890
NARRATOR: In our kitchen, Mum's pager went off before the toast did.

19
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:36,445
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

20
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:36,325
NARRATOR: She drove the lifeboat.

21
00:00:36,595 --> 00:00:39,745
[Wren and Nana at the kitchen table.]

22
00:00:36,695 --> 00:00:40,460
NARRATOR: Nana, why do you still do the book? The harbour has a computer.

23
00:00:40,730 --> 00:00:43,880
[Reverse: nana answers.]

24
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:45,400
NARRATOR: The computer wasn't here in eighteen eighty-five. The book was.

25
00:00:45,670 --> 00:00:47,930
[That evening. The Moon comes up over the harbour mouth. Wren points.]

26
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:47,810
NARRATOR: Nana, look. It's bigger.

27
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,045
[Reverse: nana answers.]

28
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:49,925
NARRATOR: Yes. It is.

29
00:00:50,195 --> 00:00:53,345
[The news: the Earth from above, the Moon’s old path dashed, its new one a little inside it.]

30
00:00:50,295 --> 00:00:58,360
NEWS ANCHOR: Observatories measure the Moon's distance every night, with lasers. Tonight it is thirty-two thousand kilometres closer than yesterday.

31
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:01,215
[Continues.]

32
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:01,095
NEWS ANCHOR: Nobody can yet say why.

33
00:01:01,365 --> 00:01:04,515
[Night. Nana at the dresser, closing the lid of the bread bin on the navy ledger. Wren, in the doorway, sees.]

34
00:01:01,465 --> 00:01:04,705
NARRATOR: That night, Nana wrote in the book for a long time.

35
00:01:04,975 --> 00:01:07,585
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

36
00:01:05,075 --> 00:01:07,465
NARRATOR: Then she locked it in the bread bin.

37
00:01:07,735 --> 00:01:10,885
[The log again: a fourth column in red pencil, headed "no moon". One entry, ringed.]

38
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:10,950
NARRATOR: On Day 3 she added a column of her own.

39
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:14,370
[Continues.]

40
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,510
NARRATOR: A small tide that came when the Moon wasn't there.

41
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:17,840
[Two shores side by side: the Moon today, and the Moon at half the distance, the sea eight times higher up the wall.]

42
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:17,720
NARRATOR: A closer Moon pulls harder on the sea.

43
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:21,140
[Continues.]

44
00:01:18,090 --> 00:01:21,705
NARRATOR: Come half as close, and the tide is eight times higher.

45
00:01:21,975 --> 00:01:25,125
[Four in the morning. Nana at the kitchen table under the lamp, covering a page with sums. Wren in the doorway in her coat over her pyjamas.]

46
00:01:22,075 --> 00:01:26,040
NARRATOR: On Day 9, Nana did sums until the kettle boiled dry.

47
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:28,645
[Reverse: wren answers.]

48
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:28,525
NARRATOR: Nana? It's four in the morning.

49
00:01:28,795 --> 00:01:31,945
[Reverse: nana answers.]

50
00:01:28,895 --> 00:01:31,965
NARRATOR: Go back to bed, love. I'm nearly done.

51
00:01:32,235 --> 00:01:35,385
[The Harbour Board’s panelled room on Market Street. Mr. Pell behind the long table; Nana standing in front of it with her hat in her hand; Wren behind her.]

52
00:01:32,335 --> 00:01:38,405
NARRATOR: It is a high spring tide, and it will settle. The Board sees no cause for alarm.

53
00:01:38,675 --> 00:01:41,825
[Reverse: nana answers.]

54
00:01:38,775 --> 00:01:42,520
NARRATOR: It won't settle, Arthur. It will come up Chapel Hill.

55
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:45,775
[Reverse: pell answers.]

56
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:45,655
NARRATOR: We've heard your floods before, Odile.

57
00:01:45,925 --> 00:01:49,075
[Laughter. Nana turns and walks out; Wren follows.]

58
00:01:46,025 --> 00:01:49,395
NARRATOR: They laughed. Nana put on her hat, and we left.

59
00:01:49,665 --> 00:01:52,815
[Wren, hanging up Nana’s oilskin, finds a folded paper in the pocket.]

60
00:01:49,765 --> 00:01:53,255
NARRATOR: On Day 12, I found a paper in Nana's coat.

61
00:01:53,525 --> 00:01:56,675
[The deeds: the ruined chapel on Chapel Hill, sold to Mrs. Odile Marsh for one hundred pounds.]

62
00:01:53,625 --> 00:01:56,815
NARRATOR: You bought the chapel? Nana, it hasn’t got a roof!

63
00:01:57,085 --> 00:01:59,245
[Continues.]

64
00:01:57,185 --> 00:01:59,125
NARRATOR: It's got a good view.

65
00:01:59,395 --> 00:02:02,545
[Night. The sea lies half a metre deep along Quay Street, lamps reflected in it; the Moon is plainly bigger.]

66
00:01:59,495 --> 00:02:03,210
NARRATOR: By Day 30, the sea came over the quay every night.

67
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,630
[The news: the Moon’s path, a little smaller again.]

68
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:07,395
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists now expect the Moon to settle within the week.

69
00:02:07,665 --> 00:02:10,815
[Continues.]

70
00:02:07,765 --> 00:02:12,060
NARRATOR: It didn't. Every promise on the news lasted about four days.

71
00:02:12,330 --> 00:02:14,180
[The kitchen, half empty: the dresser has gone, pale shapes on the wall where it stood.]

72
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:13,840
NARRATOR: Where's my bed?

73
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:16,695
[Reverse: nana answers.]

74
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:16,575
NARRATOR: Up the hill. Airing.

75
00:02:16,845 --> 00:02:19,880
[Reverse: wren answers.]

76
00:02:16,945 --> 00:02:19,760
NARRATOR: I'm not moving, Nana. This is my house.

77
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:22,440
[Reverse: nana answers.]

78
00:02:20,130 --> 00:02:22,320
NARRATOR: I know it is, love.

79
00:02:22,590 --> 00:02:25,025
[From above: the Moon racing round its smaller path. ONE MONTH = 22 DAYS.]

80
00:02:22,690 --> 00:02:24,905
NARRATOR: Even the calendar went wrong.

81
00:02:25,175 --> 00:02:28,325
[Continues.]

82
00:02:25,275 --> 00:02:30,290
NARRATOR: A closer Moon goes round faster: the month was down to twenty-two days.

83
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,710
[High water at noon: the sea stands at the door handles along Quay Street; the fishing boats float level with the windows.]

84
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:33,850
NARRATOR: By Day 60 the tides had doubled.

85
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:37,270
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

86
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:37,360
NARRATOR: Quay Street was only ours at low water.

87
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:40,780
[Low water. The harbour basin has drained: the boats lie on the mud far below the quay edge. Wren peers over.]

88
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:42,420
NARRATOR: And at low tide the sea went out so far, we could see the harbour floor.

89
00:02:42,690 --> 00:02:45,840
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

90
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:45,970
NARRATOR: Crabs, bicycles, and somebody's piano.

91
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:49,390
[Night, the top of the steps on Market Street. Mum, in her sea jacket, pointing a crewman down toward the dark water.]

92
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:49,805
NARRATOR: Mum's lifeboat went out eleven times that week.

93
00:02:50,075 --> 00:02:53,225
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

94
00:02:50,175 --> 00:02:53,440
NARRATOR: Mostly for people who had gone back for one more thing.

95
00:02:53,710 --> 00:02:55,560
[The steps of the ruined chapel, high over the town. Nana on her knees with a brush, a white line fresh across the stone.]

96
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:55,420
NARRATOR: What's the line for?

97
00:02:55,710 --> 00:02:57,560
[Reverse: nana answers.]

98
00:02:55,810 --> 00:02:57,250
NARRATOR: For later.

99
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:00,860
[The news: the Moon’s path stepping inward, a little on every circuit.]

100
00:02:57,810 --> 00:03:02,400
NARRATOR: On Day 70, a scientist said the thing nobody had thought of.

101
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:05,820
[Continues.]

102
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:10,875
NARRATOR: Nothing falls this slowly. The Moon isn't falling. Something is taking its speed away, a little on every loop.

103
00:03:11,145 --> 00:03:14,295
[Something small swings round the Moon: each loop its speed bar grows, and the Moon’s shrinks.]

104
00:03:11,245 --> 00:03:16,635
NARRATOR: Spacecraft do it on purpose: swing past a planet, and steal a little of its speed.

105
00:03:16,905 --> 00:03:19,640
[Continues.]

106
00:03:17,005 --> 00:03:19,520
NARRATOR: Something was doing it to our Moon.

107
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:22,940
[A fingertip with one grain of sand on it; beside the grain, a ring drawn round a speck too small to see.]

108
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:27,865
NARRATOR: We can't see it, because it is a black hole smaller than a grain of sand, and a third as heavy as the Moon.

109
00:03:28,135 --> 00:03:30,820
[The drowned quay at night: the Moon over the black water, the tide gauge standing in it.]

110
00:03:28,235 --> 00:03:30,700
NARRATOR: No telescope could find it.

111
00:03:30,970 --> 00:03:34,120
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

112
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:35,275
NARRATOR: The only things big enough to feel it were the Moon and the sea.

113
00:03:35,545 --> 00:03:38,695
[Inside the chapel, already half a home: camp beds, a tea urn. Wren runs in; Nana goes on sorting blankets.]

114
00:03:35,645 --> 00:03:38,685
NARRATOR: Nana! A tide with no Moon. That's your column!

115
00:03:38,955 --> 00:03:40,805
[Reverse: nana answers.]

116
00:03:39,055 --> 00:03:40,040
NARRATOR: Is it.

117
00:03:40,955 --> 00:03:43,165
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

118
00:03:41,055 --> 00:03:43,045
NARRATOR: She didn't even look up.

119
00:03:43,315 --> 00:03:45,165
[From the hill: the lower roofs standing in the sea, the lighthouse alone on its rock, the Moon half as big again.]

120
00:03:43,415 --> 00:03:44,955
NARRATOR: Day 90.

121
00:03:45,315 --> 00:03:48,465
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

122
00:03:45,415 --> 00:03:50,620
NARRATOR: The lighthouse was an island twice a day, and the school moved up the hill.

123
00:03:50,890 --> 00:03:54,040
[The news: the whole Earth from space, its oceans pulled into two bulges toward a Moon that is far too close.]

124
00:03:50,990 --> 00:03:57,910
NARRATOR: It wasn't just us. Every port on Earth closed. Whole cities moved inland, one street at a time.

125
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:01,330
[Continues.]

126
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:04,900
NARRATOR: In some bays the shape of the coast made it worse. Places that already had big tides got the biggest.

127
00:04:05,170 --> 00:04:07,380
[Night on Market Street, the Moon enormous over the roofs. Wren and Mum.]

128
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:07,260
NARRATOR: Mum? Is it going to hit us?

129
00:04:07,530 --> 00:04:10,445
[Reverse: tess answers.]

130
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:10,325
NARRATOR: No, love. I promise you it can't.

131
00:04:10,595 --> 00:04:13,745
[From above: the Moon going round and round. Far inside its path, a dashed ring.]

132
00:04:10,695 --> 00:04:15,435
NARRATOR: Mum was right. A moon doesn't fall onto its planet. It goes round it.

133
00:04:15,705 --> 00:04:18,855
[Continues.]

134
00:04:15,805 --> 00:04:23,400
NARRATOR: And one that came really close would be pulled to pieces long before it touched. Ours was never going to come anywhere near that.

135
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:26,700
[The chapel. Nana writing in the ledger; Wren, wet through from carrying boxes, finally snaps.]

136
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:26,580
NARRATOR: You care more about that book than about our house!

137
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:30,000
[Reverse: nana answers.]

138
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:30,815
NARRATOR: The book is how I know what's coming for our house, love.

139
00:04:31,085 --> 00:04:34,145
[Wren alone on the chapel wall at dusk, looking down at the drowned lower town.]

140
00:04:31,185 --> 00:04:34,025
NARRATOR: I didn't speak to her for three days.

141
00:04:34,295 --> 00:04:37,445
[Night in the chapel. Wren alone by a lantern with the navy ledger, easing two stuck pages apart.]

142
00:04:34,395 --> 00:04:38,735
NARRATOR: On Day 110, I found two pages she'd glued together.

143
00:04:39,005 --> 00:04:41,240
[The hidden page: a column of sums running down from Day 9, and at the bottom, ringed in red: last high water, 35 m. A small sketch of the chapel steps with a line across them.]

144
00:04:39,105 --> 00:04:41,120
NARRATOR: Her sum from Day 9.

145
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:44,540
[Continues.]

146
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:45,555
NARRATOR: And at the bottom, the last high tide: thirty-five metres.

147
00:04:45,825 --> 00:04:48,975
[A typed letter, folded small: Saltmouth Harbour Board, 12 November 1998. "We see no cause for alarm."]

148
00:04:45,925 --> 00:04:51,470
NARRATOR: And a letter from nineteen ninety-eight. She had warned them about a flood once before.

149
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:54,890
[Continues.]

150
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:55,130
NARRATOR: They'd written back that there was no cause for alarm.

151
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:58,535
[Nana, woken; Wren holding the open book out to her.]

152
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,415
NARRATOR: You knew. Since Day 9. Why didn't you tell them?

153
00:04:58,685 --> 00:05:01,835
[Reverse: nana answers.]

154
00:04:58,785 --> 00:05:01,805
NARRATOR: I did, love. Day 10. You were there.

155
00:05:02,075 --> 00:05:03,925
[Reverse: wren answers.]

156
00:05:02,175 --> 00:05:03,335
NARRATOR: They laughed.

157
00:05:04,075 --> 00:05:07,225
[Reverse: nana answers.]

158
00:05:04,175 --> 00:05:09,695
NARRATOR: They'll believe the sea before they believe me. So I'm getting the hill ready for when they do.

159
00:05:09,965 --> 00:05:13,115
[The opening picture again, now earned: Market Street at night, the sea lifting the yellow car.]

160
00:05:10,065 --> 00:05:13,905
NARRATOR: On Day 120, the sea came up Market Street.

161
00:05:14,175 --> 00:05:17,325
[The top of the steps on Market Street. Wren and Mum look down over the drowned roofs of Quay Street toward No. 3.]

162
00:05:14,275 --> 00:05:17,815
NARRATOR: From the steps, I watched it reach my bedroom window.

163
00:05:18,085 --> 00:05:21,120
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

164
00:05:18,185 --> 00:05:21,000
NARRATOR: Then I couldn't see my window any more.

165
00:05:21,270 --> 00:05:24,420
[Market Street by day. The sea is ankle-deep across the doorstep of the Harbour Board; Mr. Pell stands in it in his good shoes.]

166
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:24,485
NARRATOR: The next day, the sea came into the Harbour Board.

167
00:05:24,755 --> 00:05:26,605
[Reverse: pell answers.]

168
00:05:24,855 --> 00:05:26,370
NARRATOR: It will settle.

169
00:05:26,755 --> 00:05:29,520
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

170
00:05:26,855 --> 00:05:29,400
NARRATOR: He still said it. Just quieter.

171
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:32,820
[Close on the trestle in the chapel: the cups beside the tea urn, trembling in their saucers.]

172
00:05:29,770 --> 00:05:34,135
NARRATOR: Even the ground rose and fell now. Two metres, twice a day.

173
00:05:34,405 --> 00:05:37,540
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

174
00:05:34,505 --> 00:05:37,420
NARRATOR: Nobody felt it. Only the cups did.

175
00:05:37,690 --> 00:05:40,840
[Dusk on Chapel Hill. Half the town is on the grass with bundles and nowhere to go. Wren walks up to Mr. Pell with the ledger.]

176
00:05:37,790 --> 00:05:42,905
NARRATOR: Mr. Pell. This is my Nana's book. Every tide for a hundred and forty years.

177
00:05:43,175 --> 00:05:45,300
[Reverse: pell answers.]

178
00:05:43,275 --> 00:05:45,180
NARRATOR: Wren, not now.

179
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:48,600
[Reverse: wren answers.]

180
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:50,490
NARRATOR: Nana wrote down tonight's tide on Day 9. Fifteen metres. Go and look at it.

181
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,745
[Mr. Pell with the open book, reading; the sea, far below him, exactly where the pencil said.]

182
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:53,625
NARRATOR: Good Lord. She had it right.

183
00:05:53,895 --> 00:05:56,030
[Reverse: pell answers.]

184
00:05:53,995 --> 00:05:55,910
NARRATOR: She had all of it right.

185
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:58,370
[Pell turns to Nana. She points down at the white line across the step they are standing on.]

186
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:58,250
NARRATOR: Odile. Where does it stop?

187
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:00,370
[Reverse: nana answers.]

188
00:05:58,620 --> 00:05:59,435
NARRATOR: There.

189
00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:02,805
[Reverse: pell answers.]

190
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:02,685
NARRATOR: Then that's where we go.

191
00:06:02,955 --> 00:06:06,105
[The chapel, full: families on camp beds, lanterns lit. Mr. Pell, jacket off, carrying a bed frame.]

192
00:06:03,055 --> 00:06:06,445
NARRATOR: Four hundred people slept in the chapel that night.

193
00:06:06,715 --> 00:06:09,225
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

194
00:06:06,815 --> 00:06:09,105
NARRATOR: Mr. Pell carried the beds.

195
00:06:09,375 --> 00:06:12,525
[The news: the Moon’s shrinking path, and a figure.]

196
00:06:09,475 --> 00:06:14,715
NARRATOR: On Day 160, the scientists finally had a number of their own.

197
00:06:14,985 --> 00:06:18,135
[Continues.]

198
00:06:15,085 --> 00:06:22,205
NARRATOR: The visitor gets faster with every loop. Around Day 200, it will be fast enough to escape.

199
00:06:22,475 --> 00:06:25,625
[Continues.]

200
00:06:22,575 --> 00:06:27,635
NARRATOR: And the Moon should stop at about a hundred and twenty-eight thousand kilometres.

201
00:06:27,905 --> 00:06:30,060
[Wren turns from the radio to Nana.]

202
00:06:28,005 --> 00:06:29,940
NARRATOR: Nana. That's your number.

203
00:06:30,210 --> 00:06:32,060
[Reverse: nana answers.]

204
00:06:30,310 --> 00:06:31,925
NARRATOR: I rounded up.

205
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:35,360
[Night. From the chapel steps the sea is close below now, the last roofs gone, the Moon nearly three times as wide.]

206
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:38,990
NARRATOR: By Day 180, the sea was at the churchyard wall, and the Moon was nearly three times as wide.

207
00:06:39,260 --> 00:06:42,410
[Mum runs down the steps toward the water, pulling on her gloves. Wren watches her go.]

208
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:46,665
NARRATOR: Mum launched the lifeboat one last time, from a ramp cut into the hill, and brought a family back from the lighthouse.

209
00:06:46,935 --> 00:06:50,085
[Late at night in the chapel. Nana by a lantern, writing in the ledger while four hundred people sleep around her.]

210
00:06:47,035 --> 00:06:50,375
NARRATOR: That night I saw Nana writing in the book again.

211
00:06:50,645 --> 00:06:53,795
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

212
00:06:50,745 --> 00:06:55,285
NARRATOR: Not the tide. Just our names. All four hundred of them.

213
00:06:55,555 --> 00:06:58,090
[The night before. Wren cannot sleep; Nana is mending a net by the lantern.]

214
00:06:55,655 --> 00:06:57,970
NARRATOR: Nana? What if your sum is wrong?

215
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:01,390
[Reverse: nana answers.]

216
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:02,960
NARRATOR: Then we go up one more step, love. And I buy a better pencil.

217
00:07:03,230 --> 00:07:05,215
[The last night. The whole town stands along the top step, behind the white line. The sea is climbing the steps below them, bright under a Moon three times too big.]

218
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:05,095
NARRATOR: Day 200.

219
00:07:05,365 --> 00:07:08,515
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

220
00:07:05,465 --> 00:07:09,430
NARRATOR: The whole town stood on the chapel steps and watched it come.

221
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:12,850
[Close on the steps: the water lapping onto the one below the line.]

222
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,140
NARRATOR: Two steps below the line. One.

223
00:07:13,410 --> 00:07:16,560
[The water touches the white line. And lies there.]

224
00:07:13,510 --> 00:07:17,225
NARRATOR: It touched the white line. And it stopped.

225
00:07:17,495 --> 00:07:20,645
[From above: something small leaves its last loop and flies off the edge of the picture. The Moon goes on round its new, small path.]

226
00:07:17,595 --> 00:07:24,875
NARRATOR: That night, the visitor made its last loop. It had stolen all the speed it needed, and it flew off into the dark.

227
00:07:25,145 --> 00:07:28,295
[Continues.]

228
00:07:25,245 --> 00:07:31,065
NARRATOR: We thought it would take the Moon with it. It didn't. It left the Moon exactly where it was.

229
00:07:31,335 --> 00:07:34,250
[Nana holds the ledger out to Wren.]

230
00:07:31,435 --> 00:07:34,130
NARRATOR: Your book now, love. Page one.

231
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:37,060
[Reverse: wren answers.]

232
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,940
NARRATOR: Day 1. High tide: on time.

233
00:07:37,210 --> 00:07:40,270
[A bright morning a year on. Wren, a little taller, at the white line with the ledger; Nana beside her; the sea high and blue below.]

234
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:40,150
NARRATOR: We built the town again, above the line.

235
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:43,570
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

236
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:45,260
NARRATOR: The tide still climbs thirty-five metres, twice a day, and falls back.

237
00:07:45,530 --> 00:07:48,680
[Night. The two of them on the top step, small under the enormous Moon rising out of the sea.]

238
00:07:45,630 --> 00:07:49,275
NARRATOR: People ask if I miss the old Moon. I do.

239
00:07:49,545 --> 00:07:52,695
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

240
00:07:49,645 --> 00:07:53,310
NARRATOR: But nobody who has seen this one rise would give it back.

241
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:56,730
[End screen.]

242
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:57,500
NARRATOR: What would you carry up the hill first? Tell us in the comments.
